---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'fail-closed', title: 'Fail-Closed by Default' },
  { id: 'protected-paths', title: 'Protected Roots & Keep Lists' },
  { id: 'path-discipline', title: 'Absolute Paths & Log Hardening' },
  { id: 'symlink-guard', title: 'Symlinks, Mounts & Reparse Points' },
  { id: 'trust-boundary', title: 'External Definition Trust Boundary' },
  { id: 'package-guard', title: 'Package Ownership Guards' },
  { id: 'undo-engine', title: 'Backup & Undo Engine' },
  { id: 'storage-caveats', title: 'SSD, Journal & Wiping Caveats' }
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Safety Model"
  description="Deep-dive into the architectural guards, sandbox filters, and fail-closed defaults that prevent data loss and system corruption in Sweep."
  currentSlug="safety"
  toc={toc}
  prevPage={{ title: 'Residue Engine', href: '/docs/residue' }}
  nextPage={{ title: 'TOML Cleaner Authoring', href: '/docs/cleaners' }}
>
  <div class="space-y-10">
    <!-- Fail closed -->
    <section id="fail-closed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>🛡️</span>
        <span>Fail-Closed by Default</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Sweep's engine is designed so that the safe outcome is the one that happens when something goes wrong. A guard error, an unreadable path, or an ambiguous definition resolves to <em>do not delete</em>, never to a best-guess deletion. Individual failures are recorded in the report instead of aborting unrelated operations, and no cleaning pass trusts its own preview.
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-2">
        There is no single "panic-proof journal" shortcut. Safety comes from several independent guards, each of which has to pass before a path can be unlinked.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Protected paths -->
    <section id="protected-paths">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Protected Roots &amp; Keep Lists</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Sweep embeds an immutable set of kernel virtual filesystems and critical OS root hierarchies that the deletion engine strictly refuses to touch, and combines it with <em>your</em> keep list from the config:
      </p>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs font-mono">
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-bg-surface border border-border text-rust">/proc, /sys, /dev</div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-bg-surface border border-border text-rust">/boot, /boot/efi</div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-bg-surface border border-border text-rust">/etc/fstab, /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow</div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-bg-surface border border-border text-rust">C:\Windows\System32, C:\Windows\SysWOW64</div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout callout-important mt-4">
        <strong><code class="text-cyan">--force</code> cannot switch the roots off.</strong> The flag only relaxes the running-application refusal. The protected-root check has no override: even a definition that points at <code class="text-cyan">/etc</code>, <code class="text-cyan">C:\Windows</code> or your home directory itself is dropped. A dedicated test (<code class="text-cyan">protected_roots_are_never_overridable</code>) pins this down.
      </div>

      <p class="text-xs text-text-dim mt-3 italic">
        The user keep list (<code class="text-cyan">whitelist_files</code> / <code class="text-cyan">whitelist_folders</code>) acts as an unbypassable circuit breaker for every scanning pass, including <code class="text-cyan">devscan</code>, <code class="text-cyan">bigfiles</code> and <code class="text-cyan">dupes</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Path discipline -->
    <section id="path-discipline">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Absolute Paths &amp; Log Hardening</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-muted">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>No relative paths.</strong> Every filesystem action path must be absolute. A path such as <code class="text-cyan">Temp\*.log</code> — or a <code class="text-cyan">winapp2.ini</code> <code class="text-cyan">FileKey</code> whose variable is undefined — is dropped with a warning instead of being expanded against the process working directory, so running from a different folder can never delete a different set of files.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>Glob roots are preserved.</strong> A glob like <code class="text-cyan">C:\Users\foo\*.log</code> keeps its absolute drive root instead of being rebuilt as a drive-relative prefix.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>Critical-path checks are lexical.</strong> <code class="text-cyan">..</code> components are normalised before the allowlist check, so <code class="text-cyan">cleanable/sub/../../etc/passwd</code> cannot escape it.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>The operation log is opened with <code class="text-cyan">O_NOFOLLOW</code></strong> (plus an explicit reparse-point refusal on Windows). Sweep can run as root for <code class="text-cyan">apt</code>, journald and <code class="text-cyan">/var/log/nginx</code>; without this, an unprivileged user could symlink the log at <code class="text-cyan">/etc/cron.d/x</code> and have root append attacker-influenced text to it.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Symlink Guard -->
    <section id="symlink-guard">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Symlinks, Mounts &amp; Reparse Points</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        A malicious or poorly scoped cleaner could exploit a symbolic link from an unprivileged user cache directory (e.g. <code class="text-cyan">~/.cache/app/logs</code>) to a sensitive system folder. Sweep treats links, mounts and reparse points as traversal walls and refuses to follow them:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-text-muted mt-3">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>No follow-through:</strong> traversal engines never recurse into symlinked directories or Windows reparse points.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>Same-filesystem walks:</strong> deletion and glob walks stay on the target root's device, so a USB stick mounted under <code class="text-cyan">~/.cache/usb</code> is never emptied as part of a cache clean.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>Mount-point isolation:</strong> cross-filesystem mount points act as strict traversal boundaries rather than being descended into.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-cyan">▸</span>
          <span><strong>XML entity security:</strong> CleanerML XML parsing rejects DTD internal subsets and XXE expansion attacks.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Trust Boundary -->
    <section id="trust-boundary">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">External Definition Trust Boundary</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Definitions loaded from arbitrary directories, community repositories, or translated from <code class="text-cyan">winapp2.ini</code> are treated as <strong>untrusted by default</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-important">
        <strong>Automatic privilege stripping:</strong> Untrusted definitions cannot invoke executable child processes (<code class="text-rust">process</code> provider) or write arbitrary Windows Registry keys (<code class="text-rust">winreg</code> provider). These actions are removed before execution unless the user explicitly passes <code class="text-cyan">--trust-external</code> on the command line. That flag is a boundary change, not a safety guarantee — inspect the definition's source and every path before enabling it.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Package Guard -->
    <section id="package-guard">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Package Ownership Guards</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        When cleaning leftovers of uninstalled applications (<code class="text-cyan">sweep leftovers &lt;name&gt;</code>), how do we know the directory isn't currently used by a co-dependent installed package?
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-2">
        Sweep queries the local package manager database (<code class="text-cyan">dpkg-query</code>, <code class="text-cyan">rpm -qf</code>, <code class="text-cyan">pacman -Qo</code>, or Flatpak records) to verify that zero active packages claim ownership of the candidate directory before scheduling it for deletion. The Residue Intelligence Engine adds a second layer on top: a confidence score and an explicit <code class="text-cyan">safe_to_delete</code> flag, with anything below <code class="text-cyan">0.6</code> kept preview-only.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Undo Engine -->
    <section id="undo-engine">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Backup &amp; Undo Engine</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Every destructive run writes an operation record, and when <code class="text-cyan">--backup-dir</code> is set the removed files are kept so they can be restored:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust"># View past cleanup events</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep history --days 7

<span class="text-rust"># Restore files from the last session</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep undo

<span class="text-rust"># Or restore from a designated backup directory</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep undo --backup-dir ~/my-backups/sweep</code></pre>
      <p class="text-xs text-text-dim mt-3">
        The log parser is shared by <code class="text-cyan">history</code> and <code class="text-cyan">undo</code> and reads fields from the right, so entries whose <em>path</em> contains the field separator are still counted and restorable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Storage Caveats -->
    <section id="storage-caveats">
      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>⚠️</span>
          <span>Storage Hardware Limitations (SSD / NVMe / COW)</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="text-amber-100/90 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Commands like <code class="text-amber-200">sweep shred</code> and <code class="text-amber-200">sweep wipe</code> perform multi-pass data overwrites. However, on modern SSDs and NVMe drives with flash translation layers, wear leveling and over-provisioning, and on copy-on-write filesystems (Btrfs, ZFS, APFS), the controller can remap overwritten blocks. Secure shredding and free-space wiping are offered as best-effort; hardware-level full disk encryption remains the gold standard for decommissioned storage. These guards are not a substitute for backups — previews and execution are not atomic with respect to each other, permissions can change, and some system-level actions are necessarily broader than an ordinary path deletion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
